<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manikya dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1400–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manikya dynasty ruled the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about the early 15th century until 1949, one of the longest-lasting royal lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. Its kings claimed descent from the lunar dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition, and their history is told in the chronicle known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmala">Rajmala</a>. They lost the plains to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> but kept the hills, ruled as a princely state under the British and acceded to India in 1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manikya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Manikya dynasty" width="256" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Jolle at Spanish Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Tripura_Princely_State.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
